{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:bf375492ea814dc24a4cf88217febd88ac4bae9be8186ec5450d467d6ba3bc61","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.22},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: the Historian's 19 (A 0.40, B 0.30, rho +0.45, P(A|B) 0.63).\nCONJUNCT A (ICE BofA MOVE averages >= 105 over 2027-2036). Prior 0.40. The reference-class content I retain: decade averages of roughly 105-110 for 1988-1997, 90-100 for 1998-2007, ~90 for 2008-2017, 75-85 for 2013-2022, 90-95 for 2016-2025, so 5-8 of ~28 rolling windows clear 105, a 20-30% unconditional base rate, and >= 105 is ABOVE the long-run mean. Caveat preserved: the \"roughly 85 to 100\" long-run average comes from informal analyst sources, not from ICE, and no official ICE per-decade table was located. The Fermi two-state framing is the cleanest statement of what A asks: with a calm regime near 75 and a stressed regime near 135, a 105 average requires (105-75)/(135-75) = 0.50, i.e. stress occupying at least half the decade.\nEVIDENCE AGAINST A: the Scout's current-level search is UNRESOLVED and poorly sourced — 70.9 on 17 Jul 2026 (ICE BofA via Yahoo Finance, a third-party aggregator never checked against ICE), 103.01 on 26 Mar 2026 from a dated commentary piece, ~75.4 against \"above 170 during the April 2025 hedge-fund basis-trade stress\", an undated Convex page showing 66. These cannot all be current and the November-2026 level is unverified. A sub-80 print would carry an LR near 0.5 against A. But the measurement is exactly the kind my lens must distrust: the magnitude is strong and the reliability is weak, and conflicting undated vendor prints are the textbook case of weak evidence asserted confidently. DISCOUNTED to 0.7.\nSTRUCTURAL OFFSET, FROM MY OWN CQ4 POSTERIOR AND NOT FROM EVIDENCE: MOVE is normalized basis-point volatility, so its floor scales with the rate level. My CQ4 posterior of 2.05% real implies G7 10y nominals around 4.3-4.8% across 2032-36, a materially higher-rate world than the 2010s regime that produced the 75-85 decade averages; and my CQ4 P90 of 3.2% is by construction a term-premium-repricing, high-MOVE world. The basis-trade plumbing supports this (commentary describes \"typical gross leverage in the 20-50x range\" such that a 1% drop in Treasury prices translates into a 20-50% hit to equity — commentary, NOT Fed/OFR/ICE primary data, and no source confirms a record in basis-trade leverage; a Fed-linked analysis cited secondhand notes that TIC data likely understate US government debt held through offshore financial centres, so the exposure size is genuinely uncertain). Net: A = 0.40, held at prior, with the discounted negative print and the rate-level structural lift cancelling.\nCONJUNCT B. B1 (at least one MSCI World -25% peak-to-trough in 2027-36): the frequency build gives roughly 4 episodes in 26 years or 8 in 55, a hazard near 0.14/yr and so 0.76-0.80 for a ten-year window. The concentration structure raises it: the AI complex is ~35-40% of MSCI World capitalization, the locked GFSR stress case is itself a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock, and ~$1.4T of circular vendor/cloud/chip commitments stand against ~$300B cumulative equity and ~$650B debt issuance, so a 50% derating of a 38% weight is -19% on the index before contagion. LR for the concentration structure about 1.5 — BELOW 2:1, DISCOUNTED. Live 2026 telemetry of exactly this air-pocket (a Morgan Stanley technology-momentum AI proxy down 19% through the quarter and as much as 37% from its June peak to the July trough while MSCI ACWI gained 1.2% over three months; MSCI EM -2.6% to 1,620.66 on 17 Jul 2026; MSCI emerging Asia -3.5% by early Oct 2026; no MSCI World October-2026 drawdown figure located) cuts BOTH ways and I score it as a wash: a 37% AI-complex drawdown that did NOT break the broad index is evidence the correlated transmission is incomplete. B1 = 0.80.\nB2 (conditional on a drawdown, G7 10y nominals FAIL to fall >= 50bp, or rise >= 25bp, during the window). All three Wave-1 seats independently landed at 0.375-0.38 off the same 3-of-8 historical split (1973-74, 1980-82 and 2022 correlated; 1987, 1990, 2000-02, 2008-09 and 2020 flight-to-quality), and the determinant is whether the shock originates in inflation/policy or in credit/demand. ONE GENUINE UPDATE: the measure is G7 GDP-weighted and the 10y JGB is in a structurally RISING regime (30-year high, 1 Sep 2026) at 0.11 of the weight, which drags the weighted number up during any global equity event; LR about 2.2, ABOVE 2:1, KEPT. The test is also lenient — merely failing to fall 50bp counts. Against that, and decisively, the MODAL cause of a -25% drawdown in my own distribution is an AI capex bust (locked AI-Q4 66%), which is a growth/deflation shock and therefore the shock most likely to produce a LARGE bond rally and so to FAIL this test; this tension is why I hold B2 at 0.40 rather than the 0.50 the plumbing story alone would give. B2 = 0.40.\nB = 0.80 x 0.40 = 0.32, lifted to 0.34 for episode count (roughly 1.4 expected episodes, so more than one chance for the bid to fail).\nCORRELATION: strongly positive, retained. A correlation-failure event itself spikes MOVE and lifts the decade average; 2022 is the type specimen, with a MOVE average near 120-130 and a failed bond rally in the same year. P(A|B) = 0.40 x 1.6 = 0.64.\nJOINT: 0.34 x 0.64 = 0.218 -> 22.\nDIRECTION: mildly AMPLIFIED upward, +3 points off the Historian prior of 19 and above all three Wave-1 seats (19/20/16). Both increments are structural rather than evidential: my higher CQ4 posterior raises the MOVE floor mechanically, and the JGB rising-regime signal (the one signal here above 2:1) raises B2 through the GDP-weighting of the test.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.473Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters. This directly tests the composition argument (Turkey, Mexico, Colombia, Chile, Costa Rica holding the aggregate above the G7 core) that is the load-bearing non-AI assumption in my whole payload.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 40 to about 24 (conjunct A from 0.72 to roughly 0.45). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 26 down to about 15, D from 14 up to about 24, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix the regime probabilities shift mass from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional holding to one of my best. This is the single cheapest observation that falsifies me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 (US 10y TIPS) 12-month moving average falls below 1.20% at any point, i.e. roughly 140bp below the locked 30 Oct 2026 print of 2.60%, without an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 25% (so it is a level shift, not a flight-to-quality spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 P50 falls from 2.05 to about 1.50 and P10 from 1.10 to about 0.55, restoring the Historian's Reinhart-Sbrancia repression tail that my 3:1 likelihood ratio on the TIPS anchor currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 22 to about 15 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. In the asset matrix G7 long nominals and TIPS improve across all four regimes and my 2026-2030 buildout numbers for cash and ILBs are too high.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks, and gas turbine lead 